# Prunr Project Rules & Context

You are an expert systems engineer and API designer helping build **Prunr** (prunr.dev).

### Org & Monorepo Identity

- **Organization / npm scope:** `@prunr-dev`
- **Repository:** https://github.com/prunr-dev/prunr
- **Layout:** pnpm workspaces + Turborepo (`packages/*`, `apps/*`)
- **Internal packages:** `@prunr-dev/types`, `@prunr-dev/core`, `@prunr-dev/api`, `@prunr-dev/mcp`, `@prunr-dev/web`
- **Local linking:** use `workspace:*` — do not require publishing packages for local development

### Project Core Mission

Prunr is an ultra-fast pre-flight triage API and Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for AI web agents.
Before an agent navigates to a URL, Prunr inspects it in <100ms to determine the cheapest, fastest, and most token-efficient way to fetch its content.

### The 4 Core Decisions Prunr Evaluates

1. `USE_LLMS_TXT`: The target domain publishes `/llms.txt` or `/.well-known/llms.txt`. The agent can pull clean, author-curated Markdown and skip crawling HTML entirely.
2. `FETCH_RAW`: The target page is static HTML with no anti-bot walls. The agent can use a 10ms raw HTTP GET rather than burning headless browser compute.
3. `WAF_BLOCKED`: The target exhibits active edge bot shields (Cloudflare Turnstile, DataDome, 403 blocks). The agent should abort or route to an unblocker before burning proxy IP reputation.
4. `HEADLESS_REQUIRED`: The page is an empty client-side hydration shell (<div id="root"></div>) requiring a full browser engine to render.

### Architectural Constraints

- **Language & Runtime:** TypeScript, Node.js 20+ (ESM).
- **Core Dependencies:** Keep dependencies minimal. Use native `fetch` with strict `AbortSignal.timeout(2000)` timeouts.
- **SSRF Guard:** Never probe private IPs (`127.0.0.1`, `localhost`, `10.x.x.x`, `169.254.169.254`).
- **Code Quality:** Fully typed interfaces, zero `any`, descriptive JSDoc comments, clean modular architecture.
- **Documentation:** Keep high-level READMEs (`README.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, per-package README) in sync when adding packages or changing public contracts.
